      "text": "The following solution is derived from the Website https://www.safemoon.org/courses/mod-6/z-puzzle/ wikipedia: \n\nApproach #1: Use Geometric Symmetry\nSince rotations of seatings are considered identical, we can fix one person in a specific seat (e.g., the person in seat number 1) and then find the number of ways the remaining 8 people can arrange themselves around the table.\n\nThe number of ways to arrange 8 people in a line is given by $8!$ (8 factorial), which equals $40,320$.\n\nHowever, we need to account for the rotational symmetry among the table seats. Since there are 9 seats and we've fixed one person in one seat, we now consider the remaining 8 seats and the 8 possible rotations around the table.\n\nTherefore, the number of distinct seating arrangements is $8! / 9$ to account for these symmetries. This equals $40,320 / 9$, which is $4,480$.\n\nTherefore, there are $\\boxed{4,480}$ ways to seat 9 people around a round table.",
